🛒 Inventory Manager – E-Commerce Inventory System in Python

Welcome to Inventory Manager, a complete command-line E-Commerce inventory system built and designed in Python. 
this project demonstrates fundamental skills in file handling, data persistence, class design, and user interaction — 
all wrapped in a clean, modular structure that mimics real-world inventory management systems.


🚀 What It Does
This program allows users to manage a virtual product inventory with the following core features:

➕ Add New Items

📋 Display Inventory List

🎯 Filter Items by Price

❌ Delete Items by ID

💾 Save Inventory to CSV File

🚪 Exit the System


Each item is assigned a universally unique identifier (UUID) — a method widely used in SQL-based systems — to guarantee distinct, non-colliding entries.

🧠 What I Used
🧩 Key Libraries:
uuid: Automatically generates a globally unique identifier for each inventory item, ensuring data integrity and uniqueness even across large datasets.

csv: Powers the file-based storage, enabling easy read/write of inventory data across sessions.


🧱 Core Concepts:
Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)

File I/O with Exception Handling

List Comprehension for Efficient Filtering

Unique ID generation and SKU tagging

Data persistence via CSV format


💡 Why This Project Matters
This project isn’t just about storing items — it’s about learning how to build systems that mimic 
real-world processes. From product entry and deletion to persistent storage and filtering logic, 
the Inventory Manager simulates essential backend tasks of an e-commerce application in a user-friendly way.


🔍 How to Use
Simply run the program from your terminal or IDE. A text-based menu will guide you through the various 
inventory management options. Your data will automatically be saved in a local CSV file for future sessions.
